{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffBp5nEe2vtGYewqQFHS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The candidate passed the self-check (`OK`). That check didn't test convergence or novelty, and its score is weak.\n\n**The model:** `personal_side_bias_alternation_ideal`. It does what the brief asked: the change is in the decision rule, and the evidence stays simple.\n- **Decision rule:** each participant has their own habit of leaning toward the Left or Right button. It's a stable bias, separate from which sequences are shown. It adds to their impression on every trial, so it decides near-ties and pushes close calls toward the favoured side. It targets the critique's finding that participants' left-choice rates vary more than the incumbent produces (p = 0.032; q = 0.051, just short of the FDR cutoff).\n- **Evidence:** a sequence looks random to the extent that its share of H/T switches is close to the person's own ideal switching rate. Sensitivity to that distance is shared by everyone.\n\n**Check results:**\n- The short fit (100 draws, 1 chain) gave ELPD-LOO −2482.9, about 100 nats below the incumbent's −2383.0. Part of that gap may come from the short fit, but the simple evidence probably can't match the incumbent's richer terms (periodicity, heads lean, balance).\n- PSIS-LOO was flagged unreliable on that fit: 5.0% of trials have Pareto k > 0.5, against a 1% tolerance.\n- R-hat can't be computed from one chain. Admission's full fit still has to meet the convergence limits, and the novelty check runs only at admission.\n\nFiles are in `.../experiment2/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2493,"cache_read_input_tokens":63501,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2493,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_toke